    ":question/id": "8d29a6ab-40a7-524f-a945-47f820140c7a",
    ":question/problem": "Find an integrating factor depending only on $y$ for\n\n$$\ny\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-x(y^2+1),\\qquad x>0,\\ y>0\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options to complete the reasoning.\n\nFor an equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=0\n$$\n\nif\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=h(y)\n$$\n\ndepends only on $y$, an integrating factor is\n\n$$\nI(y)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nHere $\\exp(u)=e^u$.\n\nFirst rewrite the given equation in differential form:\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}\n$$\n\nLet $P$ be the coefficient of $\\mathrm{d}x$ and $Q$ the coefficient of $\\mathrm{d}y$ in that form. Compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}={{field-4}},\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}={{field-5}}\n$$\n\nThe ratio is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)={{field-6}}\n$$\n\nwhich depends only on $y$. Therefore\n\n$$\nI(y)={{field-7}}\n$$\n\nand multiplying the differential equation by this factor gives\n\n$$\n{{field-8}}\n$$\n\n",
    ":question/worked-solution": "Move the right side to the left and multiply by $\\mathrm{d}x$:\n\n$$\nx(y^2+1)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+y\\,\\mathrm{d}y=0\n$$\n\nThus $P=x(y^2+1)$ and $Q=y$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=2xy,\\qquad Q_x=0 \\\\ h(y)&=\\frac{P_y-Q_x}{P}=\\frac{2xy}{x(y^2+1)}=\\frac{2y}{y^2+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $x>0$ permits cancellation of $x$, and the ratio depends only on $y$. Use the negative integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(y)&=\\exp\\left(-\\int h(y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\right) \\\\ &=\\exp\\left(-\\int\\frac{2y}{y^2+1}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\right) \\\\ &=\\exp\\left(-\\ln(y^2+1)\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{y^2+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the integral, use $u=y^2+1$ and $\\mathrm{d}u=2y\\,\\mathrm{d}y$; also $y^2+1>0$. We choose the constant multiplier to be $1$. The exact equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\left(\\frac{1}{y^2+1}\\right)\\, [\\\\ &\\quad x(y^2+1)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+y\\,\\mathrm{d}y] =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\nx\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\frac{y}{y^2+1}\\,\\mathrm{d}y=0\n$$\n\nBoth cross-partials are $0$, verifying exactness."
  },
