    ":question/id": "ec002adb-9111-59ec-9edd-86ddca0c8fc6",
    ":question/problem": "Find an integrating factor depending only on $y$ for\n\n$$\n\\left(2xy(1-y)-x^2y\\right)\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-2y(x+y)\n$$\n\nWork on the domain $x>0$, $y>0$.\n\nRewrite the equation as $P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y=0$, with $P=2y(x+y)$. Compute the ratio and simplify before choosing:\n\n$$\n\\frac{P_y-Q_x}{P}={{field-1}}\n$$\n\nUse this ratio to choose an integrating factor:\n\n$$\nI(y)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nChoose the exact differential equation obtained after multiplying and simplifying:\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}\n$$\n\n",
    ":question/worked-solution": "Rearranging and multiplying by $\\mathrm{d}x$ gives\n\n$$\n2y(x+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(2xy(1-y)-x^2y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=0\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=2y(x+y) \\\\ Q&=2xy(1-y)-x^2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=2x+4y \\\\ Q_x&=2y(1-y)-2xy \\\\ P_y-Q_x&=2x+2y+2y^2+2xy \\\\ &=2(x+y)(1+y) \\\\ \\frac{P_y-Q_x}{P}&=\\frac{2(x+y)(1+y)}{2y(x+y)}=1+\\frac{1}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe domain makes $y$ and $x+y$ nonzero, so the cancellation is valid. The result depends only on $y$. Applying the condition on $y$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(y)&=\\exp\\left(-\\int h(y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\right) \\\\ &=\\exp\\left(-\\int\\left(1+\\frac{1}{y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\right) \\\\ &=\\exp(-y-\\ln y) \\\\ &=\\frac{e^{-y}}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe choose the nonzero constant multiplier as $1$. Multiplying and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&e^{-y}\\, [\\\\ &\\quad 2(x+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &\\quad {}+x(2-2y-x)\\,\\mathrm{d}y]=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo verify, let $\\widetilde P=2(x+y)e^{-y}$ and $\\widetilde Q=(2x(1-y)-x^2)e^{-y}$. Then\n\n$$\n\\widetilde P_y=2(1-x-y)e^{-y}=\\widetilde Q_x\n$$\n\nso the transformed equation is exact. No solution of the exact ODE is needed."
  }
